WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.833 --> 00:00:08.458
When I was 16 years old,

00:00:08.458 --> 00:00:12.167
I was getting straight As
at my fancy college prep school

00:00:12.208 --> 00:00:15.417
and feeling great about all
the possibilities ahead of me.

00:00:16.333 --> 00:00:19.875
So one day I walked into the office
of my high school guidance counselor,

00:00:19.917 --> 00:00:23.083
and I told him
about all the exciting plans

00:00:23.125 --> 00:00:25.083
that were coming together in my mind.

00:00:25.458 --> 00:00:28.958
I told him how I wanted to move
to the great city of Chicago,

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.333
where my brother was living at the time,
and start a whole new life.

00:00:32.708 --> 00:00:35.458
I told him I wanted to go
to Northwestern University

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:38.208
and build on my strengths
in something like biology

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:40.583
or piano or psychology.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:44.708
It was all just a path that felt
so right to me in my bones.

00:00:45.708 --> 00:00:48.958
The guidance counselor was this quirky
older white gentleman,

00:00:48.958 --> 00:00:50.291
I remember really well,

00:00:50.291 --> 00:00:52.916
in response, he looked directly at me

00:00:52.958 --> 00:00:54.458
and he said,

00:00:54.458 --> 00:00:56.916
"Well it's a nice plan, a great school,

00:00:56.916 --> 00:00:59.416
but it costs a lot of money

00:00:59.458 --> 00:01:01.791
and they don't let a lot of people in."

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:06.500
And that was pretty much
the end of the conversation.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:11.083
I walked out of that office knowing
I had a lot of other support in my life,

00:01:11.125 --> 00:01:13.917
I put the conversation to the side
and kept moving forward.

00:01:13.917 --> 00:01:16.000
But it stuck with me.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:20.042
And I would think about it
from time to time over the years

00:01:20.042 --> 00:01:23.417
and just wonder how
that interaction or others like it

00:01:23.458 --> 00:01:25.500
could really affect someone,

00:01:25.542 --> 00:01:28.667
especially if they don't have
a lot of other support in their life

00:01:28.667 --> 00:01:31.042
or they're not sure
what they might be able to do.

00:01:31.917 --> 00:01:34.208
So I kept going,
I followed through with my plan.

00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:35.833
It worked out really well for me.

00:01:35.833 --> 00:01:38.083
I continued on
to grad school in psychology.

00:01:38.125 --> 00:01:40.542
And eventually I found myself fixated

00:01:40.542 --> 00:01:44.583
with studying the lives
and life paths of young people.

00:01:45.375 --> 00:01:46.542
And in this work,

00:01:46.583 --> 00:01:48.792
the problem I've come to recognize

00:01:48.833 --> 00:01:52.250
is that people are constantly
made to feel smaller

00:01:52.292 --> 00:01:54.083
than they might otherwise become

00:01:54.083 --> 00:01:56.208
by the people and the systems around them.

00:01:56.667 --> 00:01:58.083
And this really matters

00:01:58.125 --> 00:02:02.708
because it holds us back from seeing
and reaching who we might be.

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:06.292
My colleagues and I
have also found, however,

00:02:06.292 --> 00:02:09.083
that key messages at critical moments

00:02:09.125 --> 00:02:12.292
can keep people inspired by possibility.

00:02:12.875 --> 00:02:15.917
We've been conducting experiments
for the past 15 years,

00:02:15.958 --> 00:02:19.833
testing how messages
that open possibilities

00:02:19.833 --> 00:02:24.083
can change people's lives
by expanding their identities,

00:02:24.125 --> 00:02:25.750
rather than shrinking them.

00:02:27.125 --> 00:02:29.000
And when I say identities,

00:02:29.042 --> 00:02:32.958
I mean, all of the ideas
that you have about who you are

00:02:32.958 --> 00:02:34.583
or who you might be.

00:02:35.542 --> 00:02:38.667
This includes the groups
that feel really core to who you are,

00:02:38.708 --> 00:02:40.167
which we call social identity.

00:02:40.167 --> 00:02:42.417
Like, your family can be
a part of your identity

00:02:42.458 --> 00:02:45.000
or your race, your ethnicity,
your economic status.

00:02:45.875 --> 00:02:49.375
But identity is more than just all these
ideas about who we are right now.

00:02:49.375 --> 00:02:52.458
It's also your ideas about who
you might become in the future,

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:54.208
which we call “future identity.”

00:02:54.542 --> 00:02:58.000
And all these different parts of identity
are constantly being shaped

00:02:58.042 --> 00:03:00.875
by the environments
that you find yourself in,

00:03:00.917 --> 00:03:04.542
by the things that you go through
and by the messages that you take in.

00:03:04.583 --> 00:03:07.208
These messages can either
shrink your identities

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:09.000
and make you feel smaller,

00:03:09.042 --> 00:03:11.000
or they can expand your identities

00:03:11.042 --> 00:03:15.167
to bring more possibilities within reach
for you to then work towards.

00:03:16.625 --> 00:03:18.917
So to make all this as clear as possible,

00:03:18.958 --> 00:03:22.292
let's imagine together a specific
11-year-old kid.

00:03:22.792 --> 00:03:23.958
Let's give him a name,

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:27.042
we can use my brother's name
and call him Reggie.

00:03:28.042 --> 00:03:29.292
At the age of 11,

00:03:29.333 --> 00:03:32.625
Reggie's moving from being
a cute little kid to a preteen.

00:03:32.667 --> 00:03:35.833
He has got good friends, loves his family.

00:03:35.875 --> 00:03:38.833
And when he gets asked that question
people always seem to ask,

00:03:38.833 --> 00:03:40.875
"What do you want to do when you grow up?"

00:03:40.875 --> 00:03:43.958
He actually has a pretty solid image
coming together in his mind.

00:03:43.958 --> 00:03:45.833
He is growing up in a modest apartment,

00:03:45.875 --> 00:03:47.250
but he loves beautiful homes

00:03:47.292 --> 00:03:50.250
and has an eye for how
they’re designed and constructed.

00:03:50.292 --> 00:03:51.458
And he likes math,

00:03:51.458 --> 00:03:55.417
so he sees himself becoming an architect
and designing homes all over the world.

00:03:56.792 --> 00:03:57.958
At the same time,

00:03:58.000 --> 00:03:59.792
he's starting to learn more and more

00:03:59.792 --> 00:04:03.625
about the barriers in his way
to reaching that possible future identity.

00:04:04.208 --> 00:04:06.292
Everything he's going
to have to accomplish,

00:04:06.292 --> 00:04:08.167
all of the money it takes to get there.

00:04:08.167 --> 00:04:11.333
It's this part that we wanted
to tackle in our research.

00:04:11.333 --> 00:04:15.750
So we've run experiments testing
how kids like Reggie react

00:04:15.792 --> 00:04:20.667
or respond to messages
that open or close possibilities.

00:04:21.125 --> 00:04:24.667
How does that change what they imagine
for their own future identities

00:04:24.708 --> 00:04:26.208
and what they actually do.

00:04:27.083 --> 00:04:29.958
So let's say, somebody comes
into Reggie's classroom one day

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.417
with a message to share.

00:04:31.458 --> 00:04:33.667
And this is just like we do
in our experiments.

00:04:33.667 --> 00:04:35.500
It's something like,

00:04:35.542 --> 00:04:37.833
"Yeah, college
can cost a lot of money.

00:04:38.167 --> 00:04:41.583
But there's this thing out there
called need-based financial aid.

00:04:41.625 --> 00:04:44.750
There are these resources
and opportunities to help you get there,

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:47.250
tuition can be really lower, even free,

00:04:47.250 --> 00:04:49.917
if you set yourself up
to get to the right places."

00:04:51.375 --> 00:04:53.917
Our experiments show
that getting that message

00:04:53.917 --> 00:04:56.625
changes how Reggie responds
to the question

00:04:56.667 --> 00:04:59.000
of "What do you see
yourself doing later in life?"

00:04:59.333 --> 00:05:01.917
All of the possibilities
that probably require college,

00:05:01.958 --> 00:05:03.250
like becoming an architect,

00:05:03.292 --> 00:05:04.708
become bigger in his mind,

00:05:04.750 --> 00:05:08.125
and he becomes 30 percent more likely
to see that for himself

00:05:08.167 --> 00:05:09.958
and give that type of response

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:12.167
compared to if he just gets
a message saying,

00:05:12.208 --> 00:05:14.292
"Here's the cost
of college, end of story."

00:05:15.375 --> 00:05:17.292
What's really interesting on top of that

00:05:17.292 --> 00:05:19.375
is that the message
that opens possibilities

00:05:19.375 --> 00:05:21.625
does more than just change
what Reggie imagines.

00:05:21.667 --> 00:05:24.333
It changes what he actually
does to get there.

00:05:24.833 --> 00:05:27.333
Our experiments show
that after getting that message,

00:05:27.333 --> 00:05:31.042
he plans to spend twice as much time
on homework the following night,

00:05:31.083 --> 00:05:33.833
and he ends up seven times more likely

00:05:33.875 --> 00:05:36.542
to actually complete
and turn in a real assignment

00:05:36.542 --> 00:05:38.792
the teacher hands out the very next day.

00:05:39.792 --> 00:05:42.583
So this is all when
he's just 11 years old.

00:05:43.125 --> 00:05:47.625
So let's follow this a little bit further
to when he's now 15 years old.

00:05:48.417 --> 00:05:50.000
He's in the middle of high school

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:53.583
and still really excited about this
possibility of architecture in his life

00:05:53.625 --> 00:05:56.833
because of the messages he's been getting,
opening the possibilities

00:05:56.833 --> 00:05:59.250
and the things he's been doing
as a result of them.

00:05:59.250 --> 00:06:02.042
At this age, he’s becoming
more and more attuned

00:06:02.083 --> 00:06:04.333
to what we call different levels of status

00:06:04.333 --> 00:06:08.250
between him and other people
and groups in society

00:06:08.250 --> 00:06:10.333
who seem to have more stuff,

00:06:10.375 --> 00:06:13.292
have nicer clothes, more expensive homes.

00:06:13.333 --> 00:06:17.958
Ultimately, he's picking up on whose lives
and identities seem to be setting them up

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:20.500
for all this success
that everybody seems to want.

00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:23.375
So what we wanted to know in our studies

00:06:23.417 --> 00:06:26.042
is how are the messages
that he's getting at this point

00:06:26.042 --> 00:06:27.583
about his own identities,

00:06:27.583 --> 00:06:30.208
who he might be now or in the future,

00:06:30.208 --> 00:06:32.917
changing the extent
to which it still feels worth it

00:06:32.958 --> 00:06:35.500
to keep working
towards this long-term goal.

00:06:36.625 --> 00:06:39.458
So let's say, somebody comes
into his high school classroom

00:06:39.458 --> 00:06:42.500
with a message to share,
could be a counselor or a teacher.

00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:44.542
Again, just like we do in our experiments,

00:06:44.542 --> 00:06:48.083
but this time it's a message
specifically about his identities.

00:06:48.708 --> 00:06:50.625
It's something like,

00:06:50.625 --> 00:06:54.500
"You may be noticing that there are
some people and groups in society

00:06:54.542 --> 00:06:57.458
who seem to have more money
or seem to be better set up

00:06:57.500 --> 00:06:58.833
to succeed in life.

00:06:59.125 --> 00:07:01.667
But actually those people and groups

00:07:01.708 --> 00:07:05.333
that are seen as having less
and having to work more for things,

00:07:05.333 --> 00:07:09.083
they gain some specific advantages
because of that experience.

00:07:09.500 --> 00:07:14.042
That type of social position leads people
to develop all sorts of strengths

00:07:14.083 --> 00:07:17.708
and skills and perspectives
and connections

00:07:17.750 --> 00:07:20.875
that are really key to contributing
to school and society."

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:25.417
What we find and replicate
in multiple experiments

00:07:25.458 --> 00:07:26.792
is getting that message

00:07:26.792 --> 00:07:30.958
that you might be a person with special
strengths because of your identity,

00:07:30.958 --> 00:07:33.667
leads Reggie to feel better
about who he is

00:07:33.708 --> 00:07:35.167
and his chances in life.

00:07:35.167 --> 00:07:38.083
And this is above and beyond
just some general positive

00:07:38.083 --> 00:07:39.667
"You can do it" type of message.

00:07:39.708 --> 00:07:43.333
It's the specific connection between
his identity and those strengths

00:07:43.375 --> 00:07:45.042
that's especially powerful.

00:07:45.583 --> 00:07:49.708
For example, he starts to reflect
and he realizes that he has to work a lot

00:07:49.708 --> 00:07:51.500
to help to contribute to the family,

00:07:51.542 --> 00:07:53.958
and he has to take care
of his brother all the time

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:55.542
while his mom is working late.

00:07:55.583 --> 00:07:57.708
So he's become really good
at time management

00:07:57.750 --> 00:07:59.458
and still getting everything done.

00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:02.500
It's these types of realizations
as a result of the message

00:08:02.542 --> 00:08:05.667
that lead him to have a significant
boost in his self-esteem

00:08:05.667 --> 00:08:07.750
compared to if he didn't get that message.

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:10.750
But it's not just that empty self-esteem.

00:08:10.792 --> 00:08:15.250
What’s again interesting
is that he becomes more inspired,

00:08:15.250 --> 00:08:17.375
and he becomes almost
10 percent more likely

00:08:17.375 --> 00:08:21.417
to continue working and persisting
every time he runs into difficulty

00:08:21.417 --> 00:08:23.833
in a challenging school task or assignment

00:08:23.833 --> 00:08:26.000
compared to if he didn't get that message.

00:08:26.333 --> 00:08:28.917
And that increase in persistence
pays off over time,

00:08:28.917 --> 00:08:33.167
it's connected to his grades staying high
or improving throughout high school,

00:08:33.167 --> 00:08:36.791
which is very different from the normal
trajectory of most students' grades

00:08:36.791 --> 00:08:39.583
slowly going down a little bit
as high school goes on.

00:08:40.583 --> 00:08:43.916
So that shift keeps his goals
within reach for longer.

00:08:43.916 --> 00:08:46.291
And it takes us to four years later.

00:08:47.125 --> 00:08:49.666
He's now 19 years old.

00:08:49.708 --> 00:08:51.125
He kept going on in school

00:08:51.125 --> 00:08:53.583
because of these messages
building up his identities

00:08:53.625 --> 00:08:55.833
and the things that he did
as a result of them.

00:08:55.875 --> 00:09:00.291
And he finds himself in this exciting
and scary new world of college.

00:09:01.125 --> 00:09:03.333
He's feeling a little bit uncertain.

00:09:03.625 --> 00:09:04.791
As he looks around,

00:09:04.833 --> 00:09:09.333
he's hearing other students use words
he's never heard before with confidence,

00:09:09.333 --> 00:09:12.500
they're talking about their trips
to Europe and all over the world.

00:09:12.541 --> 00:09:14.750
So he's really looking
for some kind of message

00:09:14.791 --> 00:09:18.291
about how his identities are seen
in this new environment.

00:09:19.416 --> 00:09:22.333
So let's say he's in
the dining hall one day early on.

00:09:22.333 --> 00:09:24.083
He doesn't know a lot of people yet.

00:09:24.083 --> 00:09:27.291
So he overhears a group
of older students sitting nearby,

00:09:27.333 --> 00:09:29.208
and they happen to be older students

00:09:29.250 --> 00:09:31.875
coming from a wide range of backgrounds.

00:09:31.916 --> 00:09:35.208
And he hears them talking
about the financial aid they're receiving

00:09:35.250 --> 00:09:37.458
and how they feel supported
at the university.

00:09:37.958 --> 00:09:39.916
He hears some of them saying they realize

00:09:39.916 --> 00:09:41.958
they're coming from homes
and neighborhoods

00:09:41.958 --> 00:09:44.458
that aren't the same as many
of the wealthy students

00:09:44.458 --> 00:09:45.625
who seem to be around.

00:09:45.666 --> 00:09:47.291
But it's turning out to help them.

00:09:47.291 --> 00:09:50.500
They know a little bit more
about how to handle the tough situations

00:09:50.541 --> 00:09:51.958
that come up during college.

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:54.958
They know a little bit more
about real-world issues and topics.

00:09:55.000 --> 00:09:58.583
He hears them say they realize
they're bringing more into the classroom

00:09:58.583 --> 00:10:01.541
because of their identities
and where they’re coming from,

00:10:01.541 --> 00:10:03.500
and that it's being recognized.

00:10:03.958 --> 00:10:08.166
Getting that message, hearing
from your peers or the institution

00:10:08.166 --> 00:10:11.125
that your identities are valued

00:10:11.125 --> 00:10:13.666
leads Reggie to feel
like he's going to figure out

00:10:13.708 --> 00:10:16.791
how to succeed in his classes
and get the most out of college.

00:10:17.208 --> 00:10:20.750
Our experiments show that getting
that message at the right time

00:10:20.791 --> 00:10:22.291
bolsters him so much

00:10:22.333 --> 00:10:24.375
that he then engages in college

00:10:24.416 --> 00:10:27.708
in ways that throughout his first year
lead his grades to improve

00:10:27.708 --> 00:10:29.833
compared to if he didn’t get that message.

00:10:29.833 --> 00:10:33.625
So much so, that he ends his first year
with grades that are just as high

00:10:33.666 --> 00:10:36.291
as students coming
from those wealthy backgrounds

00:10:36.333 --> 00:10:38.083
and well-known private schools,

00:10:38.083 --> 00:10:41.041
reducing the disparity between him
and those other students

00:10:41.083 --> 00:10:42.583
by over 60 percent.

00:10:43.791 --> 00:10:46.250
These are robust effects
that we find in experiments

00:10:46.250 --> 00:10:49.541
where we randomly assign students
to receive these messages or not.

00:10:51.208 --> 00:10:53.791
But there's a wrinkle in all of this.

00:10:53.791 --> 00:10:56.083
All of these messages, they help so much,

00:10:56.125 --> 00:10:59.458
they keep somebody seeing
and reaching towards tough goals

00:10:59.458 --> 00:11:01.500
that can pay off in life,

00:11:01.541 --> 00:11:03.000
but it can take a lot from you

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:06.125
to constantly persist
and overcome barriers.

00:11:06.791 --> 00:11:10.625
And as Reggie reaches his dream job
at a top architecture firm

00:11:10.625 --> 00:11:13.833
or in whatever field
he discovers along the way,

00:11:13.833 --> 00:11:17.541
he realizes that he's feeling
kind of stressed all the time.

00:11:17.583 --> 00:11:20.583
He's even getting physically ill
a little bit more often.

00:11:21.791 --> 00:11:23.541
This is because to make it this far,

00:11:23.541 --> 00:11:26.416
he's had to be extraordinarily
vigilant and persistent.

00:11:26.416 --> 00:11:30.291
He's had to overcome barriers
in schools and workplaces

00:11:30.333 --> 00:11:33.333
that aren't designed for people
without a lot of family wealth.

00:11:34.083 --> 00:11:37.583
He's had to deal with constant experiences
of prejudice and discrimination

00:11:37.625 --> 00:11:41.208
in settings that aren't used to
seeing someone like him succeed.

00:11:42.083 --> 00:11:45.708
We published a review of dozens
of academic articles and empirical studies

00:11:45.750 --> 00:11:49.166
showing that this type of experience
takes a toll on the physical health

00:11:49.208 --> 00:11:50.375
of people like Reggie

00:11:50.375 --> 00:11:53.833
and leads him to have an increase
in levels of inflammation in the body,

00:11:53.833 --> 00:11:57.916
which is normally a natural response
to something like a threat from a virus.

00:11:57.916 --> 00:11:59.291
But it becomes harmful

00:11:59.333 --> 00:12:02.500
and over time leads him to have
an increased risk of heart disease,

00:12:02.541 --> 00:12:04.791
cancer, chronic long-term illness

00:12:04.833 --> 00:12:07.416
compared to if he weren't
striving in this way.

00:12:08.500 --> 00:12:11.166
So in the end, is it even all worth it?

00:12:12.875 --> 00:12:15.500
Well, I do think that it is,
because our experiments,

00:12:15.541 --> 00:12:17.541
in collaboration with health research labs

00:12:17.541 --> 00:12:20.375
show that there are ways for him
to work towards these goals

00:12:20.416 --> 00:12:22.625
and maintain his health at the same time.

00:12:23.375 --> 00:12:25.208
Let's go back to when he's in college,

00:12:25.208 --> 00:12:27.291
overhearing that group
of students talking.

00:12:27.333 --> 00:12:30.666
And say he actually is able
to jump over to connect

00:12:30.708 --> 00:12:32.916
and make that new group of friends.

00:12:33.291 --> 00:12:35.916
Or he's able to join
some community or organization,

00:12:35.958 --> 00:12:37.458
like many that we work on,

00:12:37.500 --> 00:12:41.208
that's established to help him
form deep and meaningful bonds

00:12:41.250 --> 00:12:44.750
with people who may have some shared
identities and experiences

00:12:44.791 --> 00:12:47.416
and backgrounds
that they explore together.

00:12:47.916 --> 00:12:50.416
Or he's able to also
hold on to connections

00:12:50.458 --> 00:12:52.375
in the places that he calls home

00:12:52.375 --> 00:12:54.125
with the people that he calls family

00:12:54.125 --> 00:12:56.250
and with the culture that matters to him.

00:12:57.083 --> 00:13:00.125
All of these social connections
and relationships

00:13:00.125 --> 00:13:02.208
are essential for physical health.

00:13:02.875 --> 00:13:06.041
We've run experiments in collaboration
with health research labs

00:13:06.041 --> 00:13:08.708
where we randomly assign
young people to get a message

00:13:08.708 --> 00:13:10.666
that encourages them and shows them

00:13:10.708 --> 00:13:14.458
how to cultivate and maintain
these types of relationships

00:13:14.458 --> 00:13:16.333
while they're striving for these goals,

00:13:16.375 --> 00:13:17.750
taking them to new places,

00:13:17.791 --> 00:13:20.208
compared to a message
only focused on achievement.

00:13:20.916 --> 00:13:24.541
And what we find when we bring
these participants back into the lab

00:13:24.541 --> 00:13:26.416
is that over the course of a year,

00:13:26.416 --> 00:13:29.625
that increased emphasis
on connection and relationships

00:13:29.666 --> 00:13:32.875
leads to a decrease
in inflammation of 25 percent.

00:13:33.833 --> 00:13:37.041
So by not only expanding his identities

00:13:37.083 --> 00:13:40.041
but also keeping them connected
to close relationships,

00:13:40.083 --> 00:13:44.750
Reggie is able to find achievement
and health at the same time.

00:13:47.125 --> 00:13:51.666
Now my actual brother Reggie
had a very different life path

00:13:51.708 --> 00:13:55.208
than the imaginary 11-year-old kid
that we just followed.

00:13:56.250 --> 00:14:01.000
His life ended when he was struck
and killed by a drunk driver

00:14:01.041 --> 00:14:02.833
almost exactly 10 years ago.

00:14:03.333 --> 00:14:06.166
He was just a couple of years
older than I am now.

00:14:09.208 --> 00:14:12.125
I really looked up to the life
that he was able to build.

00:14:12.500 --> 00:14:17.791
It was full of joy and community
and success, doing what he loved.

00:14:18.625 --> 00:14:20.416
But he had to find all of that

00:14:20.416 --> 00:14:24.166
despite not receiving any of these
messages we've talking about in schools,

00:14:24.166 --> 00:14:27.208
building up his identities
or opening possibilities.

00:14:28.500 --> 00:14:32.583
I never had the chance to ask him
how he felt about all of that.

00:14:32.625 --> 00:14:36.708
If there were any possibilities
he felt like he'd let go of

00:14:36.708 --> 00:14:40.500
or if it all just seemed harder
than it needed to be to find his way.

00:14:43.333 --> 00:14:47.791
What if these schools and systems
that we’re in as we’re growing up

00:14:47.833 --> 00:14:50.833
actually supported everyone's
vision and potential?

00:14:51.583 --> 00:14:54.375
What if they actually
encourage people to see

00:14:54.416 --> 00:14:57.791
that there is a path to the goals
that they're working towards

00:14:57.791 --> 00:15:01.000
and that there are ways
that the circumstances of their lives,

00:15:01.041 --> 00:15:02.291
whatever they may be,

00:15:02.333 --> 00:15:04.916
are setting them up for success
as they see it,

00:15:04.916 --> 00:15:08.416
building up their identities
rather than shrinking them.

00:15:10.208 --> 00:15:12.166
These types of messages can be embedded

00:15:12.208 --> 00:15:15.500
in the ways that we teach important
and challenging topics

00:15:15.541 --> 00:15:20.625
in areas like science,
language, math, the arts, history.

00:15:21.291 --> 00:15:24.583
They can be central
to out-of-school activities,

00:15:24.625 --> 00:15:28.000
like mentoring programs
and enrichment programs.

00:15:28.333 --> 00:15:31.791
These messages
can be backed up by the policies

00:15:31.791 --> 00:15:34.791
that are necessary to make
the financial resources

00:15:34.833 --> 00:15:36.583
that you need to reach these goals

00:15:36.625 --> 00:15:41.416
actually available for every kid,
without taking on crippling debt.

00:15:43.458 --> 00:15:44.958
In the meantime,

00:15:44.958 --> 00:15:47.916
as we continue to push
for the systemic changes

00:15:47.916 --> 00:15:52.291
that would be necessary to make
these types of messages just feel common,

00:15:52.291 --> 00:15:55.458
we also each have power as individuals.

00:15:56.083 --> 00:15:58.625
I'm thinking about you as parents,

00:15:58.625 --> 00:16:04.041
as coworkers, as leaders, as friends,

00:16:04.083 --> 00:16:09.291
taking the time to show someone
that you see who they are,

00:16:09.291 --> 00:16:11.333
you see who they could be

00:16:11.375 --> 00:16:15.916
and that there is a path to the life
that they're imagining for themselves.

00:16:17.166 --> 00:16:22.583
That message from you could lead someone
to hold on to what keeps them going

00:16:22.583 --> 00:16:25.291
for just one more day.

00:16:26.458 --> 00:16:27.625
Thank you.

00:16:27.625 --> 00:16:33.541
(Applause and cheers)

